@num 5
@title Filtrations and stopping times
@titlehtml Filtrations and <em>stopping times</em>
@pages 40–42
@dek Information arrives over time, and decisions may use only what has arrived. This short chapter turns those two sentences into definitions: filtrations, adapted processes, and the random times you can act on without peeking into the future.
---
## Information that grows with time {40} [d1 i3]
<role r="roadmap" a="processes, and how information grows">
The language and tools we have developed so far lend themselves beautifully to describing sequences of random phenomena, known as **stochastic processes**. We can capture their dynamics, how we learn information about them as time passes, and their long-run behaviour.

We start with information and how it evolves. This is captured by σ-algebras (Figure 1.4 showed a σ-algebra as a body of information) and by suitable classes of random variables. We work on a fixed probability space $(\Omega,\F,\PP)$. The measure $\PP$ plays no role in this chapter; it's all about sets, functions and their measurability. $\PP$ becomes important in the next chapter, when we consider the nature of the random evolution.
</role>

<thm k="Definition 5.1" nm="Filtration" pg="40">
A <strong>filtration</strong> on the probability space $(\Omega,\F,\PP)$ is a sequence $(\F_n)_{n\ge0}$ of σ-algebras $\F_n\subseteq\F$ such that $\F_n\subseteq\F_{n+1}$ for all $n$. We then call $(\Omega,\F,(\F_n)_{n\ge0},\PP)$ a <strong>filtered probability space</strong>.
</thm>
<role r="idea" a="filtrations as growing knowledge">

Often $n$ is interpreted as time and $\F_n$ as everything that can be known by time $n$. The condition $\F_n\subseteq\F_{n+1}$ says we never forget anything: an event decided by time $n$ is still decided at time $n+1$. We usually start at time 0, but not always. We let

$$#eq19 \F_\infty=\sigma\Big(\bigcup_{n\ge0}\F_n\Big) \tag{19}$$

be the σ-algebra generated by the filtration. This captures all the information we may ever acquire, but it may be smaller than the abstract $\F$ of our space. (A union of σ-algebras is generally not a σ-algebra, hence the $\sigma(\cdot)$.)

For coin tossing, $\F_n$ is generated by the first $n$ tosses: its atoms (in the sense of Figure 1.4) are the $2^n$ possible strings of $n$ results, and each step splits every atom in two. Figure 5.1 below draws this as a tree.
</role>

<thm k="Definition 5.2" nm="Adapted stochastic process" pg="40">
A <strong>stochastic process</strong> $(X_n)_{n\ge0}$ is a sequence of random variables defined on $(\Omega,\F,\PP)$. The process is <strong>integrable</strong> if each $X_n$ is integrable. We say that $(X_n)_{n\ge0}$ is <strong>adapted</strong> to the filtration $(\F_n)_{n\ge0}$ if, for each $n$, $X_n$ is $\F_n$-measurable.
</thm>
<role r="reading" a="Definition 5.2">

We may write $X$ for $(X_n)_{n\ge0}$. If $\F_n$ represents our knowledge at time $n$, then $X$ being adapted simply means that $X_n$ is observable at time $n$: by time $n$ we know its value. Here is an obvious example of such a filtration.
</role>

<thm k="Definition 5.3" nm="Natural filtration" pg="40">
The <strong>natural filtration</strong> $(\F_n^X)_{n\ge0}$ associated with a stochastic process $(X_n)_{n\ge0}$ on the probability space $(\Omega,\F,\PP)$ is defined by $$\F_n^X=\sigma(X_0,X_1,\dots,X_n),\qquad n\ge0.$$
</thm>
<role r="reading" a="Definition 5.3">

A stochastic process $X$ is automatically adapted to the natural filtration it generates. It is also, by definition, the smallest filtration to which $X$ is adapted: "what you know if you've watched $X$ and nothing else".

The index $n$ could count days, seconds or years. It could also be some other, non-uniform clock, or something more abstract: the $n$-th letter in a book, the $n$-th word. In B8.2 you will consider continuous index sets such as $\{t\ge0\}$ instead of the discrete $\{n:n\ge0\}$ of B8.1. What matters in all these cases is that there is a linear order (which might or might not be naturally interpreted as time).
</role>

## Stopping times {41} [d2 i3]
<role r="why" a="random times that are still fair">
Whatever the interpretation of $n$, we call specific choices of $n$ **deterministic times**: $n=42$, say, or (indexing by days) the temperature recorded at Greenwich Observatory at noon on the first of July, or the Rolls-Royce Holdings plc closing price on the London Stock Exchange on a given day.

But we often use other, random times: the next time I meet you, the first time you see a yeti, the moment the stock price drops by more than 30% from its past maximum. These are well defined but not known in advance. They are of the type "I know it when it happens". We now make this precise. Much of the power of martingale methods comes from the fact that they work equally well when the index is such a random time.
</role>

<thm k="Definition 5.4" nm="Stopping time" pg="41">
Let $(\Omega,\F,\PP)$ be a probability space and $(\F_n)_{n\ge0}$ a filtration. A random variable $\tau$ taking values in $\N\cup\{\infty\}=\{0,1,2,\dots,\infty\}$ is called a <strong>stopping time</strong> with respect to $(\F_n)_{n\ge0}$ if $\{\tau=n\}\in\F_n$ for all $n$.
</thm>
<role r="reading" a="Definition 5.4">

So a random time $\tau$ is a stopping time if at any time $n$, I can use the information in $\F_n$ to decide whether to stop now ($\tau=n$) or not. Because $(\F_n)$ is a filtration, this is equivalent to $\{\tau\le n\}\in\F_n$ for all $n$ ("I stop now or have stopped already"), or to $\{\tau>n\}\in\F_n$ ("I decide to continue").<sn>Why equivalent: $\{\tau\le n\}=\bigcup_{k\le n}\{\tau=k\}$ and each $\{\tau=k\}\in\F_k\subseteq\F_n$. Conversely $\{\tau=n\}=\{\tau\le n\}\setminus\{\tau\le n-1\}$, and $\{\tau\le n-1\}\in\F_{n-1}\subseteq\F_n$.</sn> The value $\tau=\infty$ means "never stop". Think of a stopping time as a valid strategy for playing a game, investing or gambling: it can rely on information gathered so far, but can't peek into the future. All the examples before the definition have this property.

If the filtration is unambiguous we simply say $\tau$ is a stopping time; they are sometimes also called **optional times**. Not all random times are stopping times. If $n$ counts days of the year and $\tau$ is the warmest day, then I need all of $\F_{365}$ to decide when $\tau$ happened. Likewise, the day in November 2024 on which Rolls-Royce shares were most expensive is not known in advance, or even when it happens: you have to wait until the end of November. It is not a stopping time. Try it below.
</role>

@@fig5-1@@
<role r="idea" a="combining stopping strategies">

We now discuss some easy properties of stopping times. If I have two valid strategies, I may decide to stop when the first one tells me to, or when both have told me to; so the minimum and maximum of stopping times should be stopping times.
</role>

<thm k="Proposition 5.5" d="Constants, minima and maxima are stopping times" pg="41" it>
Let $(\Omega,\F,(\F_n)_{n\ge0},\PP)$ be a filtered probability space and $\tau,\rho$ stopping times. Then

<ol><li>a deterministic time $t$, $t(\omega)=n$ for all $\omega\in\Omega$, is a stopping time;</li>
<li>$\tau\wedge\rho$ and $\tau\vee\rho$ are stopping times.</li></ol>
</thm>

<sheet n="4" qs="A2">Part 2 also uses the tower property from Chapter 4.</sheet>

Here $a\wedge b=\min(a,b)$ and $a\vee b=\max(a,b)$.

<pause n="Minimum and maximum" q="Prove Proposition 5.5.">
(i) $\{t=k\}$ is $\Omega$ if $k=n$ and $\emptyset$ otherwise, and both are in every $\F_k$.

(ii) Use the "$\le$" form of the definition: $\{\tau\wedge\rho\le n\}=\{\tau\le n\}\cup\{\rho\le n\}$ and $\{\tau\vee\rho\le n\}=\{\tau\le n\}\cap\{\rho\le n\}$, both in $\F_n$.
</pause>
<role r="why" a="hitting times, the main example">

The following proposition says that the first time an adapted process enters a region is a stopping time. This **first hitting time** is the canonical example of a stopping time; many stopping times are of this type for some process. We use the usual convention $\inf\emptyset=\infty$: if the process never enters the region, the hitting time is $\infty$.
</role>

<thm k="Proposition 5.6" d="First hitting times are stopping times" pg="41" it>
Let $X=(X_n)_{n\ge0}$ be an adapted process on $(\Omega,\F,(\F_n)_{n\ge0},\PP)$ and $B\in\B(\R)$. Then $$h_B=\inf\{n\ge0:X_n\in B\},$$ the first hitting time of $B$, is a stopping time.
</thm>

<pf>
$\{h_B\le n\}=\bigcup_{k=0}^nX_k^{-1}(B)$, "$X$ has been in $B$ at some time up to $n$". Each $X_k^{-1}(B)\in\F_k\subseteq\F_n$ since $X$ is adapted.
</pf>

<pause n="One step too early" q="Toss a coin repeatedly and let $\tau$ be the first time you have seen two heads in a row. Is $\tau$ a stopping time for the natural filtration? Is $\tau-1$?">
$\{\tau=n\}$ says that tosses $n-1$ and $n$ are heads and no earlier pair of consecutive tosses was. That is decided by the first $n$ tosses, so it lies in $\F_n$ and $\tau$ is a stopping time. But $\{\tau-1=n\}=\{\tau=n+1\}$ depends on toss $n+1$: knowing the first $n$ tosses end in a head, you can't yet tell whether the next one will complete the pair. So $\tau-1$ is not a stopping time. It would require you to stop just before something happens, which is exactly what "I know it when it happens" rules out.
</pause>
<role r="caution" a="last visits are not stopping times">

By contrast, the *last* time $X$ is in $B$ before time $N$ is generally not a stopping time: to know at time $n$ that this was the last visit, you need to know that $X$ won't come back.
</role>

@@fig5-91@@

## What is known at a stopping time {41} [d3 i2]
We often use stopping times because we are interested in the value of the process at that time, the random variable $X_\tau$. Each $X_n$ is $\F_n$-measurable, since the process is adapted. But what about $X_\tau$? It is $\F_\infty$-measurable (check!), but we expect it to be measurable with respect to a smaller σ-algebra, reflecting "the information available at time $\tau$".

<thm k="Definition 5.7" d="𝓕_τ, what is known at time τ" pg="41">
Let $\tau$ be a stopping time on $(\Omega,\F,(\F_n)_{n\ge0},\PP)$. We define $$#eq20 \F_\tau=\{A\in\F_\infty:A\cap\{\tau=n\}\in\F_n\ \ \forall n\ge0\}. \tag{20}$$
</thm>
<role r="reading" a="Definition 5.7">

In words: $A$ is in $\F_\tau$ if, whenever we stop at time $n$, we know at that moment whether $A$ has happened. For example, with $\tau$ the first time a random walk hits $5$, the event "the walk visited $-3$ before hitting $5$" is in $\F_\tau$, but "the walk visits $-3$ after hitting $5$" is not. In the definition we could replace $\{\tau=n\}$ by $\{\tau\le n\}$. The following shows that this new notion behaves as we would want.
</role>

<thm k="Proposition 5.8" d="𝓕_τ is a σ-algebra, growing with τ" pg="42" it>
Let $\tau,\rho$ be stopping times on $(\Omega,\F,(\F_n)_{n\ge0},\PP)$. Then

<ol><li>$\F_\tau$ defined in <a class="xref" href="#eq20">(20)</a> is a σ-algebra;</li>
<li>if $\tau\le\rho$ then $\F_\tau\subseteq\F_\rho$.</li></ol>
</thm>

<pause n="Information at a stopping time" q="Prove Proposition 5.8.">
(i) $\emptyset\cap\{\tau=n\}=\emptyset\in\F_n$. If $A\in\F_\tau$ then $A^c\cap\{\tau=n\}=\{\tau=n\}\setminus(A\cap\{\tau=n\})\in\F_n$ (both sets are in $\F_n$). If $A_k\in\F_\tau$ then $\big(\bigcup_kA_k\big)\cap\{\tau=n\}=\bigcup_k(A_k\cap\{\tau=n\})\in\F_n$.

(ii) Use the "$\le$" form. Let $A\in\F_\tau$. Since $\tau\le\rho$, $\{\rho\le n\}\subseteq\{\tau\le n\}$, so $A\cap\{\rho\le n\}=(A\cap\{\tau\le n\})\cap\{\rho\le n\}$, an intersection of two sets in $\F_n$.
</pause>
<role r="link" a="stopped filtrations">

In particular, combining Propositions 5.5 and 5.8, $(\F_{\tau\wedge n})_{n\ge0}$ is a filtration, which is smaller than the original one in the sense that $\F_{\tau\wedge n}\subseteq\F_n$ for $n\ge0$ (as $\tau\wedge n\le n$, and $\F_n$ for the deterministic time $n$ is just $\F_n$).
</role>

## Stopped processes {42} [d2 i3]
If $(X_n)_{n\ge0}$ represents our ongoing winnings in a game and $\tau$ is our stopping strategy, then our final winnings are $X_\tau$. If $\tau<\infty$ then this is a well-defined function $$\Omega\ni\omega\longmapsto X_\tau(\omega):=X_{\tau(\omega)}(\omega),$$ and it is $\F$-measurable since $X_\tau^{-1}(B)=\bigcup_{n\ge0}\big(\tau^{-1}(\{n\})\cap X_n^{-1}(B)\big)\in\F$. In fact $X_\tau$ is $\F_\tau$-measurable. We rephrase this by introducing the stopped process.

<thm k="Proposition 5.9" nm="Stopped process" pg="42" it>
Let $X=(X_n)_{n\ge0}$ be an adapted process on $(\Omega,\F,(\F_n)_{n\ge0},\PP)$ and $\tau$ a stopping time. Then $X^\tau=(X_{\tau\wedge n})_{n\ge0}$ is a stochastic process, called the <strong>stopped process</strong>. $X^\tau$ is adapted to the filtration $(\F_{\tau\wedge n})_{n\ge0}$ and hence also to the filtration $(\F_n)_{n\ge0}$.
</thm>
<role r="reading" a="Proposition 5.9">

The stopped process follows $X$ until time $\tau$ and then freezes at the value $X_\tau$ forever.
</role>

@@fig5-92@@

<pf>
It suffices to show that if $\rho$ is a finite stopping time then $X_\rho$ is $\F_\rho$-measurable (then apply it to $\rho=\tau\wedge n$). This follows from <a class="xref" href="#eq20">(20)</a>, since $$\{X_\rho\le x\}\cap\{\rho=n\}=\{X_n\le x\}\cap\{\rho=n\}\in\F_n\qquad\text{for all }n\ge0.$$ (Also $\{X_\rho\le x\}\in\F_\infty$, as a countable union of such sets.)
</pf>
<role r="roadmap" a="where martingales begin">

Stopped processes are where the theory of martingales starts: a martingale stopped at a stopping time is still a martingale, and that one fact explains why no betting strategy can turn a fair game into a favourable one.

</role>